@node Invoking
@chapter Invoking Escape Pod
@cindex invoking

@example
escapepod daemon run [--map @var{MAP}] [--tap-timeout @var{MS}]
                     [--backend auto|evdev|x11] [--yes] [--dry-run]
                     [--only-device VVVV:PPPP]
escapepod daemon status [--socket @var{PATH}]
escapepod event monitor [--all-keys] [--map @var{MAP}]
escapepod device list
escapepod config check [--config @var{PATH}] [--map @var{MAP}]
escapepod schema [@var{noun} [@var{verb}]]
escapepod describe
@end example

The quickest start, in an X11 or Wayland session with device access:

@example
escapepod daemon run --map 'Caps_Lock=Escape' --dry-run
escapepod daemon run --map 'Caps_Lock=Escape'
@end example

The first command shows which keyboards would be grabbed and grabs
nothing; the second runs the daemon.  Tapping Caps Lock then sends Escape.
To make Caps Lock also act as Control when held, use a configuration file
(@pxref{Configuration}).

@table @code
@item daemon run
Creates the virtual keyboard, grabs every matched keyboard once no key is
down on it, and applies the mapping until it receives @code{SIGTERM} or
@code{SIGINT}.  @code{SIGHUP} reloads the configuration.  Off a terminal or
under an AI agent it needs @option{--yes}.
@option{--only-device} limits device access to a hexadecimal vendor and
product pair, such as @code{1234:5678}.  The restriction also applies after
hotplug and reload.  Identical devices may share these identifiers, so this
does not distinguish individual units.  This option requires the evdev
backend; X11 cannot enforce it.
@item daemon status
Asks the running daemon for its devices and the number of keys it holds.
@item event monitor
Prints each key event of a managed key and the decision taken (hold, tap,
used, timeout, remap).  Other keys print as @samp{other}; @option{--all-keys}
shows them too, on a terminal only.
@item device list
Lists input devices from sysfs with what the daemon would do with each:
@samp{grab}, @samp{read} (pointers), @samp{ignored}, @samp{own} (an Escape
Pod virtual keyboard) or @samp{none}.  It needs no device access.
@item config check
Validates the configuration and prints the resulting mappings; it touches
no device.
@item schema
@itemx describe
The machine-readable surface for scripts and AI agents.
@end table

Every command accepts the global flags listed in @ref{CLI interface}.  In
a pipe or under an agent, output is JSON; errors are one JSON object on
standard error with a @code{hint} to run next.  Exit codes: 0 success, 1
failure, 2 usage or configuration error, 3 not found, 4 no permission or no
backend, 5 another daemon running, 6 emergency release.

@section The emergency chord
@cindex emergency chord

Hold Backspace, Escape and Enter together to make the daemon release every
key and every grab and exit with code 6.  Use it if a mapping ever leaves a
keyboard unusable.  The service does not restart after it.  The chord is set
by @code{emergency_chord} in the configuration.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration
@cindex configuration

The configuration is a TOML file.  Escape Pod reads the path given with
@option{--config}, else @file{~/.config/escapepod/config.toml} (under
@env{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}), else @file{/etc/escapepod/config.toml}.  Check a
file before use with @code{escapepod config check}; an unknown key is an
error that names its line and column.

@verbatiminclude config.example.toml

@table @code
@item [[dual_role]]
A key with two jobs: @code{key} is the physical key, @code{hold} the key it
acts as while held (default: itself), @code{tap} the key a quick, lone tap
sends.
@item [[remap]]
A one-to-one remap from @code{from} to @code{to}, for presses, releases
and autorepeat.
@item tap_timeout_ms
How long, in milliseconds, a press may last and still count as a tap; 50
to 2000, default 200.  @option{--tap-timeout} overrides it.
@item protected
Keys never mapped.  An Orca screen-reader modifier key is protected
automatically, with a warning.
@item emergency_chord
@xref{Invoking}.
@item [[device]]
Rules for one keyboard, chosen by @code{name} (as @code{device list} shows
it) or by @code{id = "VVVV:PPPP"}.  Its @code{dual_role}, @code{remap} and
@code{tap_timeout_ms} replace the global ones; @code{ignore = true} leaves
the keyboard alone.
@item grab
@samp{all} (default) or @samp{listed}, to grab only the keyboards a
@code{[[device]]} rule names.
@end table

Key names are kernel names such as @samp{KEY_CAPSLOCK}, the same without
the prefix (@samp{capslock}), or the X keysym names xcape uses
(@samp{Caps_Lock}, @samp{Control_L}, @samp{Escape}).

@node Service
@chapter Running as a service
@cindex service
@cindex privileges

The packages install a systemd unit, @code{escapepod.service}, that runs
the daemon as the @samp{escapepod} system account.  Only that account can
read keyboards and write @file{/dev/uinput}: the udev rule grants access
to group @samp{escapepod} and no other, and no login account is added to
the @samp{input} group.  Your own programs therefore gain no ability to
read your keystrokes by installing Escape Pod.

@enumerate
@item Write @file{/etc/escapepod/config.toml}.
@item @code{systemctl enable --now escapepod.service}.
@item To read the daemon's status, add your account to @samp{escapepod-ctl}:
it grants the status socket and nothing else.
@end enumerate

On NixOS, use the module:

@example
services.escapepod = @{
  enable = true;
  package = escapepod.packages.$@{pkgs.system@}.default;
  settings.dual_role = [ @{
    key = "Caps_Lock";
    hold = "Control_L";
    tap = "Escape";
  @} ];
  statusUsers = [ "alice" ];
@};
@end example

The service has no network (@code{RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX
AF_NETLINK}) and does not restart after the emergency chord.  It reads no
user's files, so it cannot see an Orca setting: Orca users should run a
session daemon instead (@code{escapepod daemon run} from the session) or
list Orca's modifier key under @code{protected}.

@section Mouse side buttons

On the evdev backend, map a mouse button to an ordered shortcut:

@example
[[device]]
id = "1234:5678"
[[device.button]]
button = "BTN_SIDE"
keys = ["KEY_LEFTMETA", "KEY_LEFTCTRL", "KEY_RIGHT"]
[[device.button]]
button = "BTN_EXTRA"
keys = ["KEY_LEFTMETA", "KEY_LEFTCTRL", "KEY_LEFT"]
@end example

Replace the identifier with the mouse's value from @code{device list}.
Each shortcut has one to eight distinct keyboard keys.  Its keys go down
in list order and come up in reverse order when the button is released.
Global @code{[[button]]} rules also work; a per-device list replaces them.

Matched relative mice are grabbed once their buttons are up.  Motion,
wheels and unmapped buttons pass through the virtual pointer.  Devices
with unsupported capabilities, such as absolute touchpads, are left
ungrabbed with a diagnostic.  X11 rejects button rules.

When migrating from xremap, validate the configuration first, stop xremap,
then start Escape Pod.  Remove the xremap ignore rule after xremap has been
removed.  Keep only one remapper grabbing the physical devices.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting
@cindex troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item @samp{BACKEND_UNAVAILABLE} (exit 4)
No @file{/dev/uinput} access, no readable keyboard, and no @env{DISPLAY}.
Run the service (@pxref{Service}), or in an X session use
@option{--backend x11}.
@item @samp{DEVICE_BUSY}
Another program holds a device's exclusive grab.
@item @samp{POINTER_UNSUPPORTED}
The configured pointer has capabilities the virtual pointer cannot preserve.
It stays ungrabbed; use a supported relative mouse for button shortcuts.
@item Grab and release notices
@samp{DEVICE_GRABBED} and @samp{DEVICE_RELEASED} identify both keyboards
and mapped pointers entering and leaving exclusive operation.
@item Other remappers
Another program, often another remapper, holds a keyboard's grab.  Stop it,
or exclude that keyboard with a @code{[[device]]} rule.
@item A key seems stuck
Press and release it.  If the keyboard is unusable, hold Backspace, Escape
and Enter (@pxref{Invoking}).
@item Nothing happens on tap
Check @code{escapepod event monitor}: @samp{used} means another key or a
click came between press and release; @samp{timeout} means the press was
longer than @code{tap_timeout_ms}.
@item @samp{X11_REMAP_UNSUPPORTED} (exit 2)
The X11 backend cannot remap keys.  Remap with
@command{setxkbmap -option}, for example @code{setxkbmap -option
ctrl:nocaps}, and map the result as a dual-role key.
@item Unplugging a keyboard sends a stray tap
Known issue (#11): an unplug within the tap timeout of a dual-role press
taps once.
@end table

